<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Augusts Voss</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Augusts Voss was a Soviet Latvian politician who served as first secretary of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> from 1966 to 1984. Raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, he followed the line of his predecessor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arvids-pelse">Arvīds Pelše</a>, promoting industrial growth and the use of Russian. In 1984 he became chairman of the Soviet of Nationalities of the Supreme Soviet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Voss was born in Omsk Governorate, the son of a farmer, Eduard Voss, and his wife Maria. In 1933 he took a three-month teacher training course and began teaching at a primary school in the village of Znamenshchiki, and from 1935 he taught mathematics at Vikulovskaya Secondary School. After graduating from the Tyumen Teachers' Institute in 1939 he worked at a secondary school in the village of Armizon.</p><p>Called into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> in 1940, he trained as a political worker in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party of the Soviet Union</a>. From June 1942 he was commissar of an artillery battalion in the 201st Latvian Rifle Division on the Leningrad Front. He was wounded twice, and after a serious wound near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staraya-russa">Staraya Russa</a> in January 1943 he was discharged and became director of the Vikulovskaya school.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1945 Voss went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> as an instructor in the school <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the Central Committee of the Communist Party of Latvia. He studied at the Higher Party School of the Central Committee of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">CPSU</a> from 1945 to 1948 and at the Academy of Social Sciences from 1950 to 1953, and in between headed the party's science and higher education sector and the party organisation of the Latvian State University.</p><p>He then rose through the Latvian party apparatus, heading its Science and Culture Department and, from 1954 to 1960, its Party Organs Department, before serving as a secretary of the Central Committee from 1960 to 1966. He also sat on the Military Council of the Baltic Military District from 1960 to 1984.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Voss was first secretary from 15 April 1966 to 14 April 1984, which made him the actual leader of the Latvian SSR, and he was a member of the Central Committee of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">CPSU</a> from 1971 to 1990. He spoke Latvian but did not speak it in public. He chaired the Soviet of Nationalities from 11 April 1984 to 25 May 1989. He never returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 10 February 1994 and was buried at the Troyekurovskoye Cemetery. His awards included four Orders of Lenin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Augusts Voss” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Communist leaders included Arvīds Pelše and <b>Augusts Voss</b>, and a 1959 purge removed national communists.</blockquote><a href="/state/latvian-soviet-socialist-republic">Latvian Soviet Socialist Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tyumen Oblast, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=57.83,69&amp;year=1966" aria-label="Tyumen Oblast on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6025 208 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Augusts Voss"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6475 478h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6475 478h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=57.83,69&amp;year=1966">What was here in 1966? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
